    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nGH-7471 (`d5ff728`, \"Refactor int conversion into separate functions\", first released in 3.3.0) split `__Pyx_PyLong_AsSsize_t` out of `__Pyx_PyIndex_AsSsize_t`. Splitting out the unrolled multi-digit switch is a clear code-size win, but two things regressed on the hot path:\n\n1. **The compact-int fast path is gone from the wrapper.** 3.2.x returned `__Pyx_PyLong_CompactValue(b)` after a single `__Pyx_PyLong_IsCompact(b)` test. `__Pyx_PyLong_AsSsize_t` does not use those macros: for a single-digit int it computes `__Pyx_PyLong_DigitCount`, `__Pyx_PyLong_IsNeg` and `__Pyx_PyLong_Digits`, tests `size == 0`, branches to the size-1 case and applies the sign — where 3.2.x did one tagged read.\n2. **`PyLong_CheckExact` became `PyLong_Check`.** `CheckExact` compares `Py_TYPE(b)` to `&PyLong_Type`; `Check` additionally loads `tp_flags` off the type object and tests `Py_TPFLAGS_LONG_SUBCLASS`, i.e. an extra dependent load per conversion. Widening the test is a real improvement for `bool` and int subclasses (they now skip `PyNumber_Index`) and should be kept — it just needn't be paid by exact ints.\n\nThis affects every Python-int subscript of a C array or pointer.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour\n\n```cython\n# cython: language_level=3\nfrom libc.stdlib cimport calloc, free\n\ncdef class Buf:\n    cdef char* data\n    def __cinit__(self):\n        self.data = <char*>calloc(8192, 1)\n        if self.data is NULL:\n            raise MemoryError\n    def __dealloc__(self):\n        free(self.data)\n\ndef py_index(Buf b, int nargs, int reps):\n    \"\"\"One __Pyx_PyIndex_AsSsize_t per iteration.\"\"\"\n    cdef int r, k\n    cdef object idx\n    for r in range(reps):\n        idx = 0\n        for k in range(nargs):\n            b.data[idx] = 1\n            idx += 1\n\ndef py_index_two(Buf b, int nargs, int reps):\n    \"\"\"Two conversions per iteration.\"\"\"\n    cdef int r, k\n    cdef object idx, data_idx\n    for r in range(reps):\n        idx = 0\n        data_idx = 4096\n        for k in range(nargs):\n            b.data[idx] = b.data[data_idx]\n            idx += 1\n            data_idx += 4\n```\n\nControls `c_index` / `c_index_two` are the same two functions with `cdef Py_ssize_t` cursors instead of `object`, so no conversion is emitted.\n\nBuilt `-O3`, every module rebuilt from scratch with identical flags, minimum of 31 samples, five rounds with the builds interleaved in alternating order. Numbers are ns per inner iteration:\n\n| build | `py_index` | vs 3.2.9 | `py_index_two` | vs 3.2.9 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3.2.9 | 5.327 | — | 11.069 | — |\n| 3.3.0 | 5.784 | **1.086** | 12.502 | **1.129** |\n| 3.3.0 + compact | 5.524 | 1.037 | 11.320 | 1.023 |\n| 3.3.0 + compact + `CheckExact` | 5.293 | **0.994** | 11.210 | **1.013** |\n\nControls stayed flat throughout (`c_index` 0.163-0.164, `c_index_two` 0.238-0.244), and the regression is directive-independent: `py_index_two` is 1.125-1.129x across `language_level=3` alone and with `binding`, `embedsignature`, `freethreading_compatible` and all three combined.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nConversion of a small Python int should stay as cheap as in 3.2.x.\n\n### Environment\n\n- **OS:** Linux x86-64\n- **Python version:** 3.12.3\n- **Cython version:** 3.3.0 (regression also present on master)\n\n### Additional context\n\nSuggested patch — keep the delegation for the rare multi-digit case, restore the compact read, and test the exact type first:\n\n```diff\n static CYTHON_INLINE Py_ssize_t __Pyx_PyIndex_AsSsize_t(PyObject* b) {\n-    if (likely(PyLong_Check(b))) {\n+    // Exact ints dominate real call sites and cost one pointer comparison,\n+    // whereas PyLong_Check() needs a dependent load of tp_flags.\n+    if (likely(PyLong_CheckExact(b)) || PyLong_Check(b)) {\n+#if CYTHON_USE_PYLONG_INTERNALS\n+        // Handle the overwhelmingly common single-digit case directly.\n+        if (likely(__Pyx_PyLong_IsCompact(b))) {\n+            return __Pyx_PyLong_CompactValue(b);\n+        }\n+#endif\n         return __Pyx_PyLong_AsSsize_t(b);\n     } else {\n```\n\nMeasured above: the compact read recovers most of the gap, the `CheckExact` test recovers the rest (0.994x / 1.013x vs 3.2.9).\n\n**Behaviour is unchanged.** 20 cases — `bool`, int subclasses, the `__index__` protocol (including one returning a subclass and one raising), 1/2/3-digit and oversized ints, `Py_ssize_t` overflow, and non-integer types — give identical results and identical exception types under 3.2.9, unpatched 3.3.0, and both patched variants.",
