            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I first noticed this as a ~17% regression in a real extension (`cuda.bindings.driver`) while running [cuda-python's benchmark suite](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuda-python/tree/main/benchmarks/cuda_bindings) before/after a Cython 3.2.9 -> 3.3.0 bump, then bisected it down to this call site with `perf stat` (cycles +16%, instructions +7%, more legacy-decoder uops). The isolated repro above demonstrates a small part of the regression: ~0.7 ns/conversion, i.e. 3-4 cycles, inside a ~20-30 cycle loop body (I am still working on identifying the other parts).\n\nPlatform: Ubuntu 24.04, Intel Core Ultra 9 285K, gcc 13.3.0\n\nMethodology that made it visible:\n- `cpupower frequency-set -g performance`, and the benchmark process pinned to one core with `taskset -c`.\n- Builds interleaved in alternating order over 5 rounds (A, B, A, B, ...) rather than sequentially, to cancel thermal/frequency drift.\n- Mean of 31 samples.\n- Diffed the emitted C per build to confirm which code path actually ran.\n\nGiven we're on the same distro/compiler, my best guess for the discrepancy is that the effect is just small enough (~0.7 ns/conversion, 3-4 cycles) to need the pinning/interleaving above.",
